WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:06.340
The ground began to shake,

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:10.173
32 million people here in Tokyo
brace themselves for the worst.

00:00:12.001 --> 00:00:15.130
8.9 magnitude, now we
know that's a big number.

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:17.339
It was the fifth largest
earthquake in history.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.690
But the real damage comes

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:23.443
from the ferocious tsunami
that was unleashed.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:25.830
In some areas,

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.100
the waves reached 40 meters in height-

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.960
Sweeping up everything in its path

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:31.700
including cars and ships

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.350
unable to withstand the strength

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.453
and the power of the surging water.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.630
Japan is located

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.473
in the Pacific earthquake belt.

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:45.970
This means the country is struck

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:47.700
by some of the strongest earthquakes

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.303
and tsunamis in the world.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.540
In the past decade,
about 30 tropical storms

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.463
have also hit the country each year.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:03.470
These natural disasters kill
on average 100 to 200 people

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:06.783
per year and take a huge toll
on the country's economy.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:10.010
But learning from painful experience,

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.000
Japan has built one of the world's best

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.340
natural disaster response systems,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.410
which includes regular
disaster preparedness drills

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:18.943
and early warning alerts.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:21.880
Japanese engineers and architects

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.210
have also pushed the boundaries
of technology and design

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.003
to create resilient,
disaster-proof buildings.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.350
This is Tokyo Skytree.

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:42.720
At 634 meters, it's the
world's tallest tower.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.040
Designed for broadcasting and observation,

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.560
the base of Skytree occupies a
relatively small plot of land

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.883
that isn't ideal for
such a tall structure.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.210
Atsuo Konishi and his colleagues

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.220
designed a steel truss tower,

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.160
so instead of fighting against the wind,

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.053
it can simply pass through
the gaps between the trusses.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:55.430
To stabilize the tower,

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:57.760
the team used a
traditional Japanese design

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.123
called Shinbashira that
dates back over 1000 years.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.380
This is the flexible central
pillar of a Japanese pagoda,

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:07.560
which stabilizes the
tiered wooden structure

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.640
during an earthquake or typhoon.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:13.240
In a similar way to a tree
trunk supporting its branches.

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:15.150
A Shinbashira has been found at the center

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:17.210
of the world's oldest wooden structure,

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:20.183
made from a tree felled in 594 AD.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.840
For Skytree, the team
designed a 375-meter-tall

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.500
concrete core column

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.880
connected to the tower's steel outer frame

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.200
by a series of flexible oil dampers,

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.743
devices that control unwanted vibrations.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.060
The swaying of the core
column and outer frame

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.610
in opposite directions
reduce the vibration

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:45.020
of the entire tower by up
to 50% during an earthquake

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:46.783
and 30% during strong winds.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:38.340
The core column is set

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.350
on six seismic isolators made of rubber,

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:43.040
these absorb unwanted movement

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.113
and work together with the oil
dampers at various heights.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:38.740
The top part of Skytree
is a broadcasting antenna.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.060
To ensure high quality,

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:42.623
the antenna needs to stay
as stable as possible.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.450
So two tuned mass damper
systems were installed

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:48.373
to reduce unwanted vibrations.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:54.310
One is at an altitude of 625
meters and weighs 25 tons,

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.890
and the other is at an
altitude of 620 meters

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.223
and weighs 40 tons.

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:01.340
When the tower shakes,

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:04.400
these two dampers act
together as reverse pendulums,

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:07.333
reducing vibrations caused
by wind or an earthquake.

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:15.540
When Skytree was designed
in the late 2000s,

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.030
architects and engineers
flew weather balloons

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:19.970
to collect data on wind speeds

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:22.990
and build models which they
tested in a wind tunnel.

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:24.600
But for some building designs,

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.250
this expensive and time-consuming process

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:30.070
has been replaced by
faster and cheaper software

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:32.093
powered by artificial intelligence.

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:34.820
After a decade of research,

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:37.610
architectural engineering company Takenaka

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:38.920
has developed a typhoon

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.463
simulation software called Kazamidori.

00:07:29.714 --> 00:07:31.860
For a 3 kilometer square urban area,

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:34.510
it usually takes three
months to build a model,

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:37.290
conduct a wind tunnel
test and get the results,

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:39.010
but with Takenaka's software,

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:42.060
the entire process can happen
in just two or three days

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:44.150
and its engineers are
trying to reduce that down

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:44.983
to half a day.

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:35.150
Japanese architects are also experimenting

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:38.233
with unconventional building
material for highrises.

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:42.300
Skyscrapers are usually
built from concrete or steel

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:44.530
because they're strong and cost-effective.

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:47.970
But building from wood is
more environmentally-friendly.

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:50.530
Compared with the production
of steel and concrete,

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:52.710
production of timber consumes less energy

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:55.100
and releases less carbon dioxide.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:58.103
New trees can also be
planted to absorb emissions.

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:01.100
But building tall structures with wood

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:04.560
offers up its own engineering
and financial challenges.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.620
That didn't stop Japanese
construction company Maeda

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:08.856
from trying though.

00:09:57.696 --> 00:10:00.610
Maeda is going to
construct a 13-story wood

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:02.600
and steel hybrid office building

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.030
in Tokyo's Shibuya district.

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:06.910
To make the building earthquake-proof,

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:09.630
designers came up with a
hybrid lattice structure

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:10.770
that will support the walls

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:13.350
and create a more
user-friendly experience.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:18.230
These designs and technologies

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:19.610
have helped high-rise buildings

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:21.930
to withstand typhoons and earthquakes.

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:24.220
And now many low-rise buildings in Japan

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:25.970
are starting to adopt them as well.

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:29.380
Some vulnerabilities still exist,

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:31.020
like older buildings constructed

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:34.570
before regulations were tightened in 1981.

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:37.800
With natural disasters becoming
more and more frequent,

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:40.250
these innovative designs and technologies

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:43.050
could be key to protecting
the economy and lives

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:44.893
both in Japan and around the world.

